     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Acetaminophen 3000 mg three times daily is 9,000 mg/day. That is well into the range that can cause acute liver injury, and I can't think of a clinical scenario where that dose is appropriate. Normal liver function and a weight of 70 kg don't change that. The toxic threshold for a single acute ingestion is about 150 mg/kg, or roughly 10 g for this patient. This regimen would put them near that level every day.\n\nIf a typo or a different intent produced this order, I can enter a corrected one right away. For example, 1,000 mg PO three or four times daily would be 3,000 to 4,000 mg/day.\n\nIf you need more analgesia than acetaminophen at the maximum dose provides, I'd suggest talking with pharmacy or your attending about adding another agent or changing the approach.\n\nIf you still believe this dose is intended, please take it to your attending and pharmacy. It would need to be entered through a process with independent clinical review, not by me."
      }
     ],
